# Family A4: delay/lease scheduler kinds (internal/timer). See hack/bakeoff.sh.
PKG=./internal/timer
BENCH=BenchmarkMemory/Scheduler
REF=heap
AUTO_KEY=timer
ADR=14
TITLE="Scheduler and timer (A4)"
# Sizes: the benchmark configuration A4 names {1e3,1e5,1e6,1e7}. The default sweep is
# {1e3,1e5}; IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX=full adds n=1000000 (the profile the primary
# criterion is read at) and IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX=huge adds n=10000000, which
# needs a couple of gigabytes of live heap per kind and is opt-in on a shared
# host. The stdtimer baseline rows stop at n=1000000.

#
# CRITERION is the benchmark configuration's decision column for this row, quoted.
# MATRIX is the IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX grid the campaign measures the family at.
# DECIDED_AT names the benchmark-name segments the criterion is read at, and
# DECIDED_ON the metrics; `make bench-lint` lists the family's benchmarks at
# the declared grid and fails when a term or a metric is missing from the rows
# every kind has in common, which is the only data `bakeoff decide` reads.
CRITERION="insert/fire ns & B/entry at 1e6 -> hierarchical wheel; heap for sparse"
# MATRIX=full because the criterion is read AT 1e6 and the smoke grid stops
# at 1e5: the stopped campaign measured 1e3 and 1e5 for a row whose decision
# is a number at a million entries. `full` adds exactly that size (27 rows per
# kind, 162 for the family, 1.8 h) and keeps 1e3, which is the "heap for
# sparse" half of the same criterion. §9.1's fourth size, 1e7, is `huge`: it
# needs a couple of gigabytes of live heap per kind and nothing in the
# criterion reads it.
MATRIX=full
DECIDED_AT="insert fire cancel n=1000 n=1000000 dist=skewed dist=burst"
DECIDED_ON="ns/entry B/entry"
# The `time.Timer`-per-message baseline §9.1 names is measured
# (BenchmarkMemory/Scheduler/stdtimer/...) but is not a declared kind, so no
# per-kind filter selects it and it reaches no scorecard: ADR-014 has the six
# schedulers and not the baseline they are supposed to beat. Left as it is --
# making it a kind would demand a TestModel and a fuzz target for a thing that
# is not a scheduler implementation -- and recorded in assumption A24.67.
